Not compared to Slash'em, no... Anyway I have a different understanding
of munchkin than you. I suppose in a way all (at least non MMP)
computer RPGs are munchkin games, since roleplaying is impossible, and
it's really just about building up a fighting machine. The important
issues are things like how fast it goes, how features are introduced
gradually so that you go "wow! is THAT possible?", how balanced game
features are relative to each other (like classes, races, equipment
etc).
We may all be munchkins, but designers should be less so. Introducing a
massively powerful new weapon (in this case clonable slime potions)
would be rather munchkin design, but it wouldn't truly reach munchkin
propotions until the game was "rebalanced" by overpowering opponents as
well rather than reducing the unbalancing features. This forces players
to adopt the unbalancing elements, and from the look of it this has
happened in Slash'em many times - which would be why conduct play is
rare in Slash'em.

 (Incidentally, conduct play is probably the closest you get to
roleplaying in any computer RPG)



